<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.13.2: Creating Tables From Verbal Descriptions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00279</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.13.2: Creating Tables From Verbal Descriptions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8492d22c-4635-4292-b0ed-8d5a9e422c66</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity has students creating tables of values from verbal descriptions. Students will then work to use the tables of values to write linear equations. To see the connection to linear equations, students will write their own scenarios and then have a partner create a representative table of values. They will then plot the coordinates from the tables and see the linear pattern.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Ask students to think about a job they or a friend has ever had. How much did they get paid after 1 hour? Two hours? Three hours? What if they earned a $1 raise? Then what would they receive after 1 hour? Two hours? Three hours? What do you notice?</para>
<para>As students work, be sure to circulate and prompt students to work through the situations to create the next values in the table.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<title>Creating a Table of Values</title>
<para>For each situation in numbers 1 – 3, create a table of values with at least five columns to represent the situation.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Julio works at a clothing store and makes $14 per hour. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">Number of Hours</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">Amount Earned</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>14</entry>
      <entry>28</entry>
      <entry>42</entry>
      <entry>56</entry>
      <entry>70</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<list start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Jake had cavities at his last dentist visit. The dentist told him to brush his teeth for two minutes each time he brushes. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">Amount Earned</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">Total Number of Minutes Brushed</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>6</entry>
      <entry>8</entry>
      <entry>10</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<list start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> For a fundraiser, Celia ran 5 miles on Monday (day 0) and then 3 miles every day after that. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></emphasis> </para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
      <entry>8</entry>
      <entry>11</entry>
      <entry>14</entry>
      <entry>17</entry>
      <entry>20</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>Using a Table of Values to Write Equations</title>
<para>Table of values can be used to write equations. The tables from <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis> all represent linear equations. </para>
<para>For questions 4 - 8, revisit the table from question 1 and write an equation to represent the table of values. Notice that columns for the \(x\) and \(y\) values are added. </para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>14</entry>
      <entry>28</entry>
      <entry>42</entry>
      <entry>56</entry>
      <entry>70</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the starting value (when \(x = 0\), what does \(y\) equal)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 0\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> How would you describe this value on the graph? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y\)-intercept</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the rate of change, or the change between the \(y\)-values over the change between the \(x\)-values? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac{14}{1}\) or 14</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> How would you describe this value on the graph? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> slope</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> Using the information in questions 4 – 7, what would the equation be for the values represented by the table? Use slope-intercept form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y= 14x +0\) or \(y = 14x\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write an equation from the table of values created in question 2 of <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis>.</item>
</list>
<para>Hint: (Work backward to find the value where \(x = 0\) using the pattern set in the table of values).</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>?</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>6</entry>
      <entry>8</entry>
      <entry>10</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 2x\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write an equation from the table of values created in question 3 of <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis></item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
      <entry>8</entry>
      <entry>11</entry>
      <entry>14</entry>
      <entry>17</entry>
      <entry>20</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 3x +5\)</para>
<title>Video: Writing an Equation from a Table of Values</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing equations from a table of values.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen="" frameborder="0" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/nZiRu4PpXDo?si=OEfKjtDOzQiG_BR2;&amp;rel=0" title="YouTube video player"/></iframe>
</div>
<title>Create Your Own Situation</title>
<para>Think of your own situation that could be modeled by a linear equation. Create a table of values that represents that situation. Write your problem down then switch with a partner. Make a table of values for each other’s problems. Then, check your work together. </para>
<para>Next, plot the points of each table of values in a coordinate plane. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Describe the scenario created by your partner. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Answers vary.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use Desmos to graph the table of values to represent your partner’s scenario. (Students were provided access to Desmos). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Answers vary.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Does your partner’s data model a linear relationship or not? Describe how you determined your answer. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The points form a line. This happens because the same number was added to each value in the top row and the same number was added to each value in the bottom row of the tables.</para>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Kylie pays $20 to rent a scooter plus $0.40 per mile she drives the scooter. Create a table of values to represent Kylie’s situation starting with \(x=0\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">Miles driven, \(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">Cost, \(y\)</entry>
      <entry>20</entry>
      <entry>20.40</entry>
      <entry>20.80</entry>
      <entry>21.20</entry>
      <entry>21.60</entry>
      <entry>22.10</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the table to determine how much she has to pay after 5 miles. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Kylie would pay $22.10.</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>When writing an equation from a table, students often switch the slope and \(y\)-intercept in the slope-intercept form of an equation. Continue to emphasize the \(y\)-intercept is the starting value when \(x=0\) and the slope is the rate of change.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Have pairs of students share their situations and matching tables of values. Discuss what shape the graphs are all taking and why. As students plot their points, they should recognize that a linear pattern forms. Help students connect the linear pattern to there being the same slope between points.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Conversing, Listening<title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Conversing, Listening</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>As they write their situations, monitor ELL students to ensure that they learn relationships between sounds and letters of the English language to represent sounds when writing in English.</para>
    <para>As they share and discuss with partners, have ELL students practice producing sounds of newly acquired vocabulary such as long and short vowels, silent letters, and consonant clusters to pronounce English words in a manner that is increasingly comprehensible in vocabulary words such as slope and intercept.</para>
    <para>During collaboration with partners, monitor ELL students to ensure that they are distinguishing sounds and intonation patterns of English with increasing ease. They should recognize elements of the English sound system in newly acquired vocabulary such as long and short vowels, silent letters, and consonant clusters. Additionally, they need to learn relationships between sounds and letters of the English language and decode (sound out) words using a combination of skills such as recognizing sound-letter relationships and identifying cognates, affixes, roots, and base words in words such as linear, graphing, and plotting.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Optimize output; Cultivate conversation</para>
  </div>
</div>
<title>1.13.2: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which of the following equations could be represented by this table of values?</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>20</entry>
      <entry>23</entry>
      <entry>26</entry>
      <entry>29</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y = 20x\)</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The \(y\)-intercept is 20, not the slope. The answer is \(y = 3x +20\).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y = \frac{1}{3}x + 20\)</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope is found by the change in \(y\) over the change in \(x\). The answer is \(y = 3x +20\).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y = 3x +20\)</entry>
      <entry>That’s correct! Check yourself: The \(y\)-intercept, when \(x = 0\), is at 20. The slope, or change in \(y\) over change in \(x\), is \(\frac31\) or 3.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y =20x +3\)</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This rule does not work for each column in the table. The answer is \(y = 3x +20\)</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>1.13.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Writing Linear Equations from Tables</title>
<para>When writing linear equations from tables, it is important to identify the slope and the \(y\)-intercepts from the table.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>Given the table, write an equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>12</entry>
      <entry>16</entry>
      <entry>20</entry>
      <entry>24</entry>
      <entry>28</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
<para>The \(y\)-intercept is the starting value, when \(x=0\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope.</para>
<para>Slope is the change in \(y\) over the change in \(x\).</para>
<para>The slope is \(\frac41\) or 4.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para>Slope intercept form is \(y = mx +b\), where \(m\) is the slope and \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
<title>Try It: Writing Linear Equations from Tables</title>
<para>Given the table, write an equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>6</entry>
      <entry>11</entry>
      <entry>16</entry>
      <entry>21</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Compare your answer:</para>
<para>Here is how to write the equation in slope-intercept form:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
<para>The \(y\)-intercept is the starting value, when \(x=0\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope.</para>
<para>Slope is the change in \(y\) over the change in \(x\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para>Slope intercept form is \(y = mx +b\), where \(m\) is the slope and \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
</section>
